(function(){try{
(()=>{'use strict';
const pages={
"/":`<section id="sr-editorial-depth" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">ABOUT SPIN RAIDERS</p><h2>A UK arcade guide built around real places, real machines and useful trip planning</h2><p>Spin Raiders is designed to help people decide where to go, what kind of venue they are looking at and how that stop can fit into a bigger day out. The directory covers traditional seaside arcades, family entertainment centres, adult gaming centres, bowling and arcade venues, holiday-park arcades, classic-machine locations and other places where amusement gaming forms part of the visit. Venue pages are not intended to be a list of names and addresses: each guide is being built around practical visitor information, the type of experience on offer and the surrounding destination.</p><p>The strongest pages are backed by first-hand Spin Raiders visits, original photography, videos, machine sightings or clearly identified public sources. Where information is uncertain, the site separates confirmed details from items that still need checking rather than presenting guesses as fact. Machine line-ups are especially date-sensitive, so a cabinet seen on one visit should never be treated as permanently available. The same applies to opening hours, promotions and facilities, which can change without notice.</p><p>Use the arcade directory when you want to compare individual venues, the destination guides when you are planning a wider trip, and the classic fruit-machine archive when you are trying to identify a particular cabinet or title. The map brings those parts together geographically. Spin Raiders also links relevant nearby attractions so an arcade visit can sit alongside food, shopping, sightseeing, family activities or another entertainment stop instead of being the only thing on the itinerary.</p><h3>How to use the information</h3><p>Start with the venue type and age guidance, then check the address, travel notes and any verified facilities. If a page names machines, treat that as a recorded sighting rather than a guarantee for today. For family trips, check which parts of a venue are all-ages and whether any adult gaming area is separate. For adult gaming centres and bookmakers, the surrounding-area notes are there to help you combine a short visit with other town-centre plans. If something has changed, use the report-a-change option so it can be checked and corrected.</p><p>The aim is simple: make the site useful enough that somebody can plan a real visit from it, not simply land on a page created to hold advertising.</p></section>`,
"/arcades":`<section id="sr-editorial-depth" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">UK AMUSEMENT ARCADES</p><h2>How to choose an arcade that suits the trip you are planning</h2><p>UK arcades cover far more than one type of venue. A traditional seaside arcade may mix 2p pushers, cranes, ticket redemption, video games and fruit machines. An adult gaming centre is an 18+ environment built around regulated gaming machines. Bowling centres and multi-activity venues may have an arcade alongside lanes, food and other bookable activities, while holiday parks often place their amusement area inside a much larger resort. Spin Raiders keeps those differences visible so visitors can compare like with like.</p><p>For families, the useful questions are whether the venue is genuinely family-focused, what other activities are nearby, how long a visit is likely to fill and whether food, toilets, parking or accessibility information has been verified. For classic-machine fans, current sightings matter more than broad claims such as “retro arcade”. Machine floors change, cabinets move and jackpot packages differ, so the individual venue guide should be read together with the date and evidence behind any reported line-up.</p><p>Location matters as much as the machines. Seaside resorts such as Blackpool, Cleethorpes, Skegness, Scarborough and Southend can support a full arcade crawl with food and attractions in between. Town-centre gaming venues may make more sense as a shorter stop combined with shopping or another activity. Use the location and nearby-attraction links on each guide to build a realistic plan rather than travelling solely on the strength of one old machine report.</p><h3>What Spin Raiders verifies</h3><p>Where possible, pages distinguish current operator information, public premises records, first-hand visits, public ratings and historical machine evidence. Ratings are kept separate from Spin Raiders assessments, and missing public scores are not replaced with invented numbers. Venue photos should represent the actual place, while destination imagery should represent the wider town or resort. If a detail cannot be supported, it should be marked as unverified rather than dressed up as certainty.</p></section>`,
"/map":`<section id="sr-editorial-depth" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">PLAN BY PLACE</p><h2>Using the Spin Raiders UK arcade map</h2><p>The map is intended as a planning tool, not just a field of pins. Use it to see which venues sit close enough to combine in one trip, compare different types of entertainment in the same area and move from a map result into the full venue or destination guide. A pin can represent a traditional arcade, adult gaming centre, bowling venue, casino, bingo club, bookmaker, family attraction or another relevant leisure stop, so check the category before travelling.</p><p>For seaside trips, zoom out far enough to see the whole resort rather than choosing the first arcade on the promenade. For city visits, the map can help combine a gaming venue with shopping, museums, restaurants or other attractions already held in the directory. For motorway and service-area gaming, treat the stop primarily as part of a journey and check the correct carriageway, parking rules and service facilities.</p><p>Addresses and map positions are useful starting points, but businesses move, rebrand and close. If a pin appears wrong, open the linked guide and use the report-a-change option. The directory is designed to improve through checked corrections rather than silently leaving stale locations in place.</p></section>`,
"/general-1-1":`<section id="sr-editorial-depth" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">UK BOOKMAKERS DIRECTORY</p><h2>Betting-shop listings with useful town-centre context</h2><p>Spin Raiders includes bookmakers because many high streets and leisure areas visited for arcade trips also contain licensed betting shops. These pages are not presented as family attractions or amusement arcades. They are adult gambling premises and should be planned accordingly, with age restrictions and current operator information checked before a visit.</p><p>A useful bookmaker guide needs more than the brand name. The practical value is knowing where the branch sits in relation to the rest of the town: nearby shopping streets, food, parking, public transport and other stops that can make sense before or after a short visit. Where a branch is in a busy centre, the surrounding-area section is intended to help visitors fit it into errands or a broader day out rather than travel solely for the premises.</p><p>Opening hours, services and promotions can change by branch, and individual gambling products should never be assumed from an old listing. Spin Raiders therefore treats current operator information and licensing records as stronger evidence than copied directory text. If a branch has closed, moved or changed operator, report the change so the record can be checked and updated.</p><p>Gambling is for adults. The directory describes physical premises and visitor context; it does not guarantee outcomes, winnings or the continued availability of any particular product.</p></section>`,
"/forum":`<section id="sr-editorial-depth" class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">SPIN RAIDERS COMMUNITY</p><h2>Share current arcade information without turning rumours into facts</h2><p>The community area gives visitors a place to discuss arcade trips, machine sightings, classic fruit machines and changes at venues. The most useful posts are specific: name the venue, town, approximate date and machine or facility you are talking about. A recent photograph or first-hand visit is more useful than repeating an old list from another website.</p><p>Machine sightings can become outdated quickly. A cabinet reported today may be moved, converted or removed later, so community information should be treated as a lead until it is checked. The same applies to opening times, ownership changes and facilities. When a correction affects a directory page, use the report-a-change route as well as discussing it in the forum so the underlying venue record can be reviewed.</p><p>The community complements the directory rather than replacing it. Venue and destination pages contain the structured visitor information; discussion threads add current experience, memories and leads that can help keep those guides accurate.</p></section>`
};
function mount(){
 const p=(location.pathname||'/').replace(/\/$/,'')||'/';
 const html=pages[p]; if(!html||document.getElementById('sr-editorial-depth'))return;
 const root=document.getElementById('sr-seaside-root')||document.getElementById('PAGES_CONTAINER')||document.body;
 const main=root.querySelector('main')||root;
 const foot=root.querySelector('footer');
 const box=document.createElement('div');box.innerHTML=html;const node=box.firstElementChild;
 if(foot)foot.before(node);else main.append(node);
}
let tries=0;const timer=setInterval(()=>{mount();if(document.getElementById('sr-editorial-depth')||++tries>40)clearInterval(timer)},100);
addEventListener('pageshow',mount);addEventListener('popstate',()=>setTimeout(mount,100));
})();
}catch(e){console.warn('SR snippet failed: Spin Raiders Editorial Depth 20260918',e)}})();

